.\" Manual page of the radiolack command, installed by the Debian package.
.\" packaging/build_deb.py fills in the version and the date.
.TH RADIOLACK 1 "@DATE@" "Radiolack @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
radiolack \- receive\-only SDR receiver with band plans
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B radiolack
[\fB\-\-ui\fR \fIwindow\fR|\fIbrowser\fR|\fInone\fR]
[\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR]
[\fB\-\-rtl\-sdr\fR [\fIDEVICE\fR] | \fB\-\-rtl\-tcp\fR [\fIADDRESS\fR]]
.br
.B radiolack
\fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Radiolack shows the spectrum and the waterfall of an SDR receiver in a
desktop window and plays NFM, AM, USB, LSB and CW.
It only receives: it has no transmit function.
It reads an RTL\-SDR receiver plugged into this computer, or one that an
rtl_tcp server serves from another computer, and labels every frequency
with a simplified European band plan, with the IARU Region\ 1 plans
inside the amateur bands.
.PP
Without \fB\-\-rtl\-sdr\fR or \fB\-\-rtl\-tcp\fR, Radiolack opens the
source used last: the simulated 2\ m band the first time.
The Source panel switches between sources.
Closing the window stops Radiolack; without a window, stop it with
Ctrl+C.
.PP
Radiolack serves its interface on 127.0.0.1 only, so other devices cannot
reach it, and answers only requests addressed to 127.0.0.1 or localhost.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-ui\fR \fIwindow\fR|\fIbrowser\fR|\fInone\fR
Show the interface in a desktop window (the default), in the default web
browser, or nowhere: Radiolack then only prints the interface's address.
When the window cannot open, for instance without a graphical display,
Radiolack says why, keeps running and prints the address.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fIPORT\fR
Listen on \fIPORT\fR, on 127.0.0.1, instead of any free port.
.TP
\fB\-\-rtl\-sdr\fR [\fIDEVICE\fR]
Read the RTL\-SDR receiver plugged into this computer, through an rtl_tcp
that Radiolack runs while an interface is open.
\fIDEVICE\fR is the receiver's index or serial number: 0, the first one,
by default.
rtl_tcp comes with the rtl\-sdr package.
.TP
\fB\-\-rtl\-tcp\fR [\fIADDRESS\fR]
Read an RTL\-SDR receiver through an rtl_tcp server that you run, at
\fIADDRESS\fR: \fIHOST\fR, \fIPORT\fR or \fIHOST\fR:\fIPORT\fR.
By default, the address used last: 127.0.0.1:1234 the first time.
The Source panel can change it.
Not with \fB\-\-rtl\-sdr\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/radiolack/settings.json\fR
The settings of each receiver and the interface's preferences.
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/radiolack/bookmarks.json\fR
The bookmarks.
.PP
Without \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR, or when it is not an absolute path, the
folder is \fI~/.config/radiolack\fR.
A file that Radiolack cannot read is renamed aside, never overwritten, and
Radiolack starts from its defaults.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 once Radiolack stops, after its window closes or on Ctrl+C or SIGTERM;
1 when it cannot start, for instance on a port already in use;
2 for an invalid option.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rtl_tcp (1)
